reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The journey:

Step sequence:
1. seek #open-help (maybe they clicked)
2. press Enter -> opens help panel (#help-text becomes visible)
3. press Tab -> expected focus on #help-close but actually focus went to #amount (the input field outside the panel).
Thus the issue: When opening the offcanvas (help panel), focus should be trapped inside the panel, moving to the first focusable element (likely #help-close button) then tabbing stays within panel. Instead focus goes to amount input which is behind the panel (outside modal).

Observed state shows after step 2 (Enter) the help panel opened, focus was on #help-close? Actually after step 2 they pressed Enter on #open-help, leading to panel open. Then step 3: pressing Tab from #help-close? Wait the observed failed step says:

Failed step index 3: action {"key":"Tab","kind":"press"} with before having focus on #help-close (button) and after focus moved to #amount (input). So indeed after opening panel, focus is on #help-close button (makes sense because offcanvas likely sets focus to first focusable element? maybe it focuses the trigger? But they got focus on close button). Then pressing Tab moved focus to #amount, which is outside the panel, meaning focus trap not working.

Thus the bug is in the focus trap implementation (FocusTrap) or offcanvas initialization not activating trap correctly when scroll config is false? Let's examine offcanvas code.

In offcanvas.js, show method:

```
show(relatedTarget) {
    if (this._isShown) { return; }
    const showEvent = EventHandler.trigger(this._element, EVENT_SHOW, { relatedTarget });
    if (showEvent.defaultPrevented) { return; }
    this._isShown = true;
    this._backdrop.show();
    if (!this._config.scroll) {
      new ScrollBarHelper().hide();
    }
    this._element.setAttribute('aria-modal', true);
    this._element.setAttribute('role', 'dialog');
    this._element.classList.add(CLASS_NAME_SHOWING);
    const completeCallBack = () => {
      if (!this._config.scroll) {
        this._focustrap.activate();
      }
      this._element.classList.add(CLASS_NAME_SHOW);
      this._element.classList.remove(CLASS_NAME_SHOWING);
      EventHandler.trigger(this._element, EVENT_SHOWN, { relatedTarget });
    };
    this._queueCallback(completeCallBack, this._element, true);
}
```

Note: focus trap activation is conditional on `!this._config.scroll`. In the HTML, the offcanvas element has attributes: `data-bs-scroll="true"` (look at index.html: `<div class="offcanvas offcanvas-end" data-bs-scroll="true" data-bs-backdrop="true" tabindex="-1" id="help" aria-labelledby="help-title">`). So scroll is true. Therefore `!this._config.scroll` is false, so focus trap is NOT activated. That explains why focus can escape.

Thus the bug: For offcanvas with scroll=true, they didn't activate focus trap, causing focus to leak. According to WCAG, modal dialogs should trap focus regardless of scroll setting. The offcanvas should treat as dialog and trap focus.

Thus fix: Activate focus trap regardless of scroll config (or at least when role dialog). According to bootstrap spec, offcanvas with scroll still should trap focus? Probably yes. So we need to modify the show method to always activate focus trap (or at least when not static?). Let's see if there is any reason they conditionally activate trap only when !scroll. Maybe they thought when scroll enabled, the user can interact with background scrolling? But still focus trap needed.

Thus edit offcanvas.js: In show method, change the condition to always activate focus trap (remove the if). Or maybe activate when !this._config.scroll || this._config.scroll? Actually just always.

